;;; 00d-loading.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Chapter 00d · loading
;;;
;;; Two ways to bring a library's verbs into your program: eager and lazy.
;;; Written 2026-07-22. Applies to Motoi too.

(chapter :number "00d" :library "loading" :training-eligible #t
  :prose
"# Loading libraries — eager and lazy

Motoi Scheme has hundreds of verbs. Not every program needs all of them. The runtime lets you choose HOW they get loaded — up front (eager) or when you first reach for them (lazy).

## The two modes

**Eager** — the verb is ready before your first line runs. Every eager library installs at boot. Boot costs a few milliseconds. Every call after that is full speed.

**Lazy** — the library's file is parsed at boot but its verbs are wired the first time you touch one. Boot is nearly instant. First call to any of that library's verbs pays about 1 ms. Every call after that is full speed.

## Which one to use

Games and animations should pick **eager**. A game loop can't afford a stutter when a rarely-used verb fires for the first time on frame 47. Eager everything up front — your first frame is fast and every frame after stays fast.

Scratchpads, one-off scripts, and experiments should let **lazy** do its thing. Startup is instant. You can reach for whatever verb you want without pre-declaring it. The first-touch millisecond is invisible to you.

**Don't feel guilty about lazy.** Programs are scratchpads until you know what they are. That's how you figure them out. Once your program does what you want, look at what you actually used, flip those libraries to eager, and ship. The rest stay lazy.

## Calling a lazy library on demand

You can force a lazy library to install without waiting for the first call:

```scheme
(require 'matrix)          ; install lib/math/matrix.js right now
(require 'num-integrate)   ; install lib/math/num-integrate.js right now
```

Handy at the top of a game loop's setup phase — pay the millisecond in setup, not in the frame budget.

Two more verbs for inspection:

```scheme
(loaded-libs)              ; every library that has actually installed so far
(available-libs)           ; every library the manifest knows about
```

## Where the mode lives

Each library file declares its own mode with a single flag. You don't edit the runtime to change a library from lazy to eager — you change one line in the library itself, save, run.

Libraries live in `lib/` inside the Motoi source tree, organized by category (`lib/math/`, `lib/game/`, `lib/graphics/`, etc.). Every file that ships with Motoi is registered in `core/verb-manifest.js`. If a library isn't in the manifest, it doesn't load — dropping a broken file into `lib/` will not crash Motoi for anyone else. Registration is intent.

## Writing your own library

The pattern is documented in `engineering/HOW-TO-REGISTER-A-VERB.md`. Short version: create a file, export an `install` function plus a small metadata block (`VERBS`, `LAZY`, `BOOK`, `TESTS`), and add one line to the manifest.

## Motoi Scheme

The sister dialect is a variant of Motoi. It uses the same loader and the same mode flags. If you're reading the sister dialect's reference and see the same three verbs (`require`, `loaded-libs`, `available-libs`), they behave identically."

  :verbs (
    (:name "require"        :signature "(require lib-name-symbol) -> ok | unknown"
     :summary "Install a lazy library by name. Returns 'ok if it installed (or was already loaded), 'unknown if no library matches."
     :example "(require 'matrix) ; → ok")
    (:name "loaded-libs"    :signature "(loaded-libs) -> list-of-symbols"
     :summary "List of libraries that have actually installed so far in this session."
     :example "(loaded-libs) ; → (base/assert math/matrix)")
    (:name "available-libs" :signature "(available-libs) -> list-of-symbols"
     :summary "List of every library the manifest knows about, whether loaded or not."
     :example "(available-libs) ; → (ai/ai ai/steering audio/tick base/alg ...)")))
